WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.880
Riddled with graphic sensuality and violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:09.080
Saltburn left a serious mark on the 2023 film&nbsp;
scene to its very last frame. This is the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.000
about Saltburn's shocking conclusion,&nbsp;
and beware, there are spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.240
Saltburn opens in what appears&nbsp;
to be roughly the present day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.080
with Oliver Quick reflecting on his relationship&nbsp;
with Felix Catton. The film then flashes back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.160
when the two first met at Oxford in 2006.&nbsp;
Like most students at the university,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.560
Felix comes from wealth, whereas Oliver is&nbsp;
one of the few students there on scholarship.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.760
Supposedly Oliver's alcoholic father has&nbsp;
passed away and Oliver has no interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.520
in coming home to his drug-addicted&nbsp;
mother. Feeling bad for his new friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.200
Felix invites Oliver to spend the summer&nbsp;
at Saltburn, his family's lavish estate.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.320
At Saltburn, Oliver is introduced to Felix's&nbsp;
family: his absent-minded father Sir James,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.840
his snobby mother Elspeth, and his depressed and&nbsp;
bulimic sister Venetia. Also living at Saltburn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.960
is Felix's cousin, Farleigh Start. Farleigh is&nbsp;
a biracial American who's desperate to cling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.800
his British family's status and already has an&nbsp;
antagonistic relationship with Oliver at school.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.040
Oliver is obsessed with Felix, and when&nbsp;
Venetia and Farleigh both try to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.680
it clear to him that Felix just sees&nbsp;
him as a temporary "toy" to play with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.720
Oliver responds by messing with&nbsp;
both of them in perverse ways.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.040
In the final act of the film, the Catton family&nbsp;
prepares a big birthday bash for Oliver themed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.760
around A Midsummer Night's Dream, but Felix&nbsp;
becomes wise to Oliver's manipulations. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.440
morning before the party, Felix takes Oliver&nbsp;
on a surprise visit to Oliver's parents' house.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.440
Contrary to Oliver's sob stories, both of his&nbsp;
parents are alive, friendly, middle class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.840
and seemingly not addicts. At the party, Felix is&nbsp;
put off by Oliver's dishonesty and doesn't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:46.440
be anywhere near him. Even so, Oliver tracks him&nbsp;
down in Saltburn's labyrinth to declare his love.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:51.040
After the party, Felix's body is found dead&nbsp;
in the labyrinth. Sir James and Elspeth try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.160
to act like everything's okay, but Venetia and&nbsp;
Farleigh can't keep up the ridiculous facade.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.440
"What? You want me to just sit here&nbsp;
and eat it like nothing's happening?"

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.779
"What else is there to do, darling?"

00:01:59.779 --> 00:02:00.680
"Anything!"

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.840
After the funeral, Oliver essentially has sex with&nbsp;
Felix's grave. Not long after her brother's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.760
Venetia commits suicide, and while&nbsp;
Elspeth wants to keep Oliver around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.560
Sir James wants to pay their&nbsp;
guest to leave Saltburn.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.520
Years pass, and the film brings us back&nbsp;
to the present day. Sir James dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:21.520
and an older Oliver reunites with Elspeth. She&nbsp;
welcomes him back to Saltburn with open arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.760
but sure enough, she falls ill&nbsp;
and ends up on life support.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.080
It's revealed that Oliver's opening&nbsp;
narration was being delivered to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.000
incapacitated Elspeth. Oliver confesses that&nbsp;
he killed Felix by poisoning his drink before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.520
ripping away Elspeth's life support. With the&nbsp;
whole Catton family destroyed, Saltburn is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.280
in Oliver's possession. He celebrates by doing&nbsp;
cocaine and dancing naked around the building.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.120
The premise of Saltburn makes it seem easy&nbsp;
to compare it to the recent wave of dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.800
satires about the haves and have-nots,&nbsp;
such as Parasite or The Menu. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.440
Saltburn finds material to laugh about in the&nbsp;
eccentric foibles and uncomfortably callous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:01.320
attitudes of the ultra-wealthy Cattons. Its&nbsp;
attitude toward the upper class feels a lot less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.000
vicious than other films of this type. The reason&nbsp;
for this is largely because its ultimate villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.920
isn't a rich person or a greater systemic force.&nbsp;
Instead, it's Oliver, a middle-class interloper.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.960
Whatever sympathy Oliver might initially engender&nbsp;
as an outsider is lost due to both his twisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.920
actions and lack of psychological relatability.&nbsp;
Viewing Saltburn as a commentary on class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.640
the fact that Oliver lies about being poor&nbsp;
and struggling when he is really just envious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.460
of those who have more than he does might&nbsp;
even come across as a reactionary message.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.760
Nonetheless, some critics have still described&nbsp;
the film as an "eat the rich'' movie. The logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.200
here is that, even if Oliver comes off as&nbsp;
more sociopathic than his wealthy hosts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.440
people can still find enjoyment in seeing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.360
slowly destroy those silly out-of-touch&nbsp;
Cattons and claim Saltburn for himself.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.400
It's important to acknowledge that&nbsp;
Saltburn takes place in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.280
where issues of class are treated differently&nbsp;
than they are in America. While American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.280
capitalism is based on the idea of "the&nbsp;
American dream," where anyone from any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.040
background can make it big if they work hard&nbsp;
enough, British class structures are treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.200
with a much more rigid line between casts. This is&nbsp;
particularly visible in institutions like Oxford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.560
where a scholarship student like&nbsp;
Oliver completely sticks out.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.800
"I mean, you're almost passing."

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.680
"For what?"

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.600
"I don't know?"

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.760
In that cultural context, it's possible&nbsp;
Brits may feel even greater catharsis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.080
seeing Oliver find ways to rise above his&nbsp;
station, regardless of the twisted methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.120
through which he goes about it. In America,&nbsp;
however, stories of people attaining massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:29.880
amounts of wealth through controversial&nbsp;
methods are both common and popular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:35.080
so this aspect of the film plays a bit more&nbsp;
basic and less richly satisfying across the pond.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.120
Throughout the film, Saltburn acknowledges this&nbsp;
culture clash through the American character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.760
of Farleigh. One of the more complex supporting&nbsp;
characters in the film, Farleigh bullies Oliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.560
to assert his connections to privilege within the&nbsp;
British class structure, but Farleigh's position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.840
is very much conditional and his hyper-privileged&nbsp;
family still treats him as an outsider.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.480
There's no doubt this is&nbsp;
due to his biracial status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.600
for while class hierarchies are slightly&nbsp;
less ingrained in the American psyche,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.000
racial hierarchies are fixed in&nbsp;
place on both sides of the Atlantic.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.320
While Saltburn invokes a lot of&nbsp;
conversations about social class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.760
the main source of drama in the film&nbsp;
is Oliver's feelings for Felix. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.440
film's opening narration, Oliver claims he&nbsp;
"loved" Felix but wasn't "in love" with him.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.720
Both Oliver and Felix talk of "love"&nbsp;
for each other throughout the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.640
and at least in Oliver's case, that goes&nbsp;
beyond seeing Felix as good friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:29.480
into a clear homoerotic sexual fixation. In the&nbsp;
end, Oliver declares he actually hated Felix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.160
and that his feelings largely drove&nbsp;
him to wreak havoc on the Catton clan.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.800
Of course, there's much to debate over&nbsp;
what exactly Oliver considers "love."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.480
He comes across as a complete sociopath,&nbsp;
lacking empathy towards others and showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:45.360
no hesitation about cruelly manipulating&nbsp;
the people around him. For this reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.240
he may be entirely incapable of&nbsp;
love as it's traditionally defined.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.520
Whether or not his feelings can be accurately&nbsp;
called "love," however, he's certainly filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.600
passion and obsession. In addition to the sexual&nbsp;
attraction he feels for Felix, much of his desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:04.080
for his friend is rooted in envy. Oliver wants&nbsp;
Felix's social status, wealth, and his effortless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.880
ability to charm everyone around him. In the&nbsp;
end, Oliver is able to take all of Felix's former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.640
belongings for his own, but gaining his victim's&nbsp;
personal strengths is another matter entirely.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.160
With such a psychologically ambiguous protagonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.720
Saltburn generally aims to shock and intrigue&nbsp;
more than build a deeper connection between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.080
audience and its characters. Even so, there&nbsp;
is one scene towards the end of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.880
that stands out by letting more genuine&nbsp;
emotion enter the picture. What's more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.320
the sequence also doubles as the film's most&nbsp;
pointed joke about the British upper class.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.935
The morning that Felix has been found dead, Sir&nbsp;
James and Elspeth still refuse to let go of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.935 --> 00:06:43.440
preexisting plans despite their son's death.&nbsp;
Gathered at the dining room table, the parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.400
just want to talk about how great Oliver's&nbsp;
birthday party was while eating meat pies.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.080
In contrast, Farleigh and Venetia react&nbsp;
to the death with actual tears. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:55.040
Farleigh tries to talk about how absurd and&nbsp;
cruel it is to act as if nothing happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.880
the parents respond with orders to quietly eat his&nbsp;
lunch and not taint the meal with his emotions.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.480
"None of us wants your bloody American feelings."

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.920
The parents represent the stoic attitude&nbsp;
to "keep calm and carry on" to a comically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:11.880
unhealthy extreme. Even outside the specific&nbsp;
cultural context, this scene hits hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.800
due to the universal struggle to pretend&nbsp;
things are normal in the face of tragedy.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.600
Given the conscious need to avoid spoilers&nbsp;
about the film's many twists, the cast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:26.040
crew of Saltburn haven't spoken directly about&nbsp;
the film's ending as of the end of 2023. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.560
Emerald Fennell has commented on the general&nbsp;
themes of the film, offering some helpful insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.680
into how to process the film's ending in addition&nbsp;
to the topics she doesn't outright comment on.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.480
When speaking to Vogue, the filmmaker&nbsp;
avoided answering questions about the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:43.000
the film addresses racism, sexism, and classism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.520
deeming these political issues secondary&nbsp;
to her actual main focus. She explained,

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.440
"Really, it's a film about first love.&nbsp;
Generally, because I'm quite facile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.800
I think everything has to do with&nbsp;
sex, and I think our fetishization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.280
of the country house and titles&nbsp;
is completely sadomasochistic."

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:02.960
She went on to discuss some of the complexities&nbsp;
of humanity that inspired the film, saying,

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.520
"I'm utterly obsessed with how we relate&nbsp;
to things that we want and desire and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:11.320
also kind of hate and know are unattainable —&nbsp;
things that we know will never love us back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.440
whether that's a person or a house or a&nbsp;
culture. And yet we can't f---ing stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.240
being desperately attracted to&nbsp;
them. My question is, 'Why?'"

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.720
Imagery from Oliver's climactic A Midsummer&nbsp;
Night's Dream birthday party has been present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:27.680
throughout the marketing for Saltburn, so&nbsp;
while the dramatic events of the party are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.160
still spoiler material, the film's crew has been&nbsp;
able to speak about the design elements of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.480
sequence. In the press notes for the film,&nbsp;
production designer Suzie Davies explained,

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.800
"There were so many design elements&nbsp;
that we wanted to bring to the party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:42.760
elements that needed to feel like&nbsp;
they'd been in the family for years."

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:46.320
Davies went on to explain the choice to&nbsp;
include the fairly out-of-place image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.560
of a pig roasting on a spit&nbsp;
at the lavish affair, saying,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.200
"And then to contrast that, we talked about having&nbsp;
the slightly grotesque pig on the spit rolling in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.280
front of it. It was all about the juxtaposition&nbsp;
of the surreal, the sinister, and the colorful."

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.920
Costume designer Sophie Canale also&nbsp;
opened up about Oliver's antlered costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.360
explaining that it came from a desire&nbsp;
to make him look mythical. She also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.400
pointed out the contrast between&nbsp;
the intensely jeweled designs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.320
Venetia and Elspeth's costumes against&nbsp;
Felix's lazier Puck costume, saying,

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.240
"You've got everyone else at the party&nbsp;
in ballgowns, black tie, fancy dress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.920
or trashy dresses. But Felix is&nbsp;
just in his jeans and a vest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.160
and then he's put a pair of wings on and&nbsp;
that's all the effort he's going to make."

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.640
Speaking in the press notes before the SAG-AFTRA&nbsp;
strike, Archie Madekwe, who played Farleigh in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.760
the film, described the dramatic effect&nbsp;
the party has on the story. He observed,

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:37.720
"Throughout the film, there's something&nbsp;
of a heightened Shakespearean quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:41.920
to Saltburn and the Cattons and then&nbsp;
it all comes to life on this night."

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:46.000
Another key to understanding Saltburn is&nbsp;
its connections to gothic romance and horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:51.240
stories. Fennell cited several gothic novels&nbsp;
like Daphne du Maurier's Rebecca as influences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.320
in the film's press notes. According to&nbsp;
The Standard, at screenings of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:00.120
she's gone further in describing the movie's&nbsp;
horror as being akin to a vampire flick, saying,

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.080
"Certainly metaphorically it is a vampire&nbsp;
film. It is about what we do when we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.400
completely besotted with something&nbsp;
or someone. And I hope it's part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:11.680
the classic Gothic tradition where love&nbsp;
and hate are very, very close together."

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.080
While discussing some elements of the film's plot&nbsp;
after a screening at the Academy Museum in Los&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:21.360
Angeles, Fennell went further into how the vampire&nbsp;
metaphor applies to Oliver. According to Deadline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.840
her comments highlighted how Oliver tends to,&nbsp;
quote, "absorb the qualities of life he desires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:29.520
from those around him the way a vampire&nbsp;
sucks a mortal's blood." For their part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.000
the Cattons do the same to other characters in&nbsp;
the film. Possibly the clearest example of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:39.320
behavior is Elspeth's conflicting treatment of&nbsp;
her troubled friend Pamela throughout the film.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:40.979
"Pamela died?"

00:10:40.979 --> 00:10:41.779
"Yeah."

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:44.480
"She'd do anything for attention."

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.800
As reported by Gold Derby, Fennell explained her&nbsp;
biggest goal for the film following another awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:55.240
consideration screening in Los Angeles. Speaking&nbsp;
in a Q&amp;A moderated by Variety's Clayton Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.040
she said that she wants each person who&nbsp;
views the film to feel something about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.720
even if they don't outright&nbsp;
enjoy it. She explained,

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:06.000
"Hate it, love it, be turned on, be freaked out&nbsp;
[...] whatever it makes you feel. I want people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.440
to feel something. That's the reason you make&nbsp;
a movie and not a TV show or write a book."

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.360
Speaking with MovieMaker, Fennell elaborated that&nbsp;
the large amount of nudity and sexual scenarios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.000
throughout the film were also intended to invoke&nbsp;
an unexpected reaction. She explained that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.000
on-screen nudity is about, quote, "grief or&nbsp;
triumph," as opposed to eroticism. For this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.640
reason, the regular sex scenes in the movie&nbsp;
are focused mainly on the actor's faces. while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.200
the actual nudity comes at more emotionally&nbsp;
cathartic points of the film such as Oliver's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:39.040
behavior at Felix's grave or his victorious&nbsp;
dancing after he claims Saltburn for his own.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:43.440
Whether Fennell successfully conveyed these&nbsp;
ideas to her audience is up to the viewer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:47.400
but with some of the most shocking scenes&nbsp;
and imagery to hit the screen this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:52.124
Saltburn is sure to bring about a reaction&nbsp;
in just about anyone who puts it on.

